# =============================================================================
# mbsync configuration template for protonmail-local-ai
# =============================================================================
# This file is a template — environment variables are substituted at container
# startup by envsubst. Do not put real credentials here — committed to git.
#
# Variables substituted at runtime (see render_mbsync_config in entrypoint.sh):
#   ${MBSYNC_IMAP_HOST}    — the name Bridge's certificate is checked against
#                            (BRIDGE_CERT_HOST)
#   ${MBSYNC_IMAP_CONNECT} — a Tunnel to BRIDGE_HOST:BRIDGE_IMAP_PORT, the
#                            Bridge app on the host
#   ${BRIDGE_USER}         — Bridge IMAP username (from the Bridge app's
#                            IMAP details)
# =============================================================================

# IMAP Account — connection to the Proton Mail Bridge app's IMAP server.
# PassCmd avoids credentials appearing in docker inspect or process list.
# TLSType IMAPS: implicit TLS (RFC 8314, Bridge's "SSL" mode, #638). The
# TLS handshake is the first thing on the connection, so there is no
# plaintext phase to strip or inject into, and isync never falls back to
# STARTTLS or plaintext. Bridge serves it on the IMAP port once the
# Bridge app's IMAP connection mode is set to SSL. It must be set: with a
# Tunnel, isync otherwise defaults to no TLS. (isync 1.5 renamed
# SSLType to TLSType; the old name still works but prints a notice on
# stderr every run, which the entrypoint would count as an error.)
# Leave TLSVersions unset so isync/OpenSSL can negotiate the strongest
# supported protocol instead of pinning the connection to TLS 1.2.
# CertificateFile: extracted automatically at container startup by entrypoint.sh.
IMAPAccount protonmail
Host ${MBSYNC_IMAP_HOST}
${MBSYNC_IMAP_CONNECT}
User ${BRIDGE_USER}
PassCmd "cat /run/secrets/bridge_pass"
TLSType IMAPS
CertificateFile /tmp/mbsync/bridge-cert.pem

# Remote store — maps IMAPAccount to a named store for use in Channel.
IMAPStore protonmail-remote
Account protonmail

# Local store — where emails are written in Maildir format.
# SubFolders Legacy: a top-level folder is the directory of that name, and
# each child is its parent's directory plus "/." and the child's name, so
# Folders/Clients/cur is /maildir/Folders/.Clients/.cur. A child can then
# never land on its parent's own cur, new or tmp directory, as it did with
# SubFolders Verbatim (#281), and names keep their dots, which
# SubFolders Maildir++ refuses. The indexer reads the folder name back
# from the path (indexer/src/parser.py _derive_folder).
MaildirStore protonmail-local
Path /maildir/
Inbox /maildir/INBOX
SubFolders Legacy

# Channel — connects remote Bridge IMAP to local Maildir.
# Patterns excludes All Mail and Labels/*. All Mail is a virtual view that
# Bridge's operation guard refuses to change. Labels are excluded by project
# policy, not by a Bridge restriction: the index does not model label
# membership apart from physical folders, so a label copy would add
# duplicates it could not describe. Upstream Bridge source reviewed at
# commit b9c5dac (point-in-time evidence, not a pin: Bridge updates are
# managed by the app) does not restrict label mailboxes. Expunge None
# avoids the IMAP CLOSE error that Gluon reports under Expunge Both or
# Far (gluon#426).
# It excludes the top-level Starred folder too, which is also virtual: a
# second copy of each starred message, whose star already arrives as the
# F flag on the real copy (#692). A custom folder named Starred is
# Folders/Starred and still syncs.
# It also leaves out a child folder, and everything below it, named after
# a file isync keeps in its parent's directory: under SubFolders Legacy
# its directory would be that file (.uidvalidity, .isyncuidmap.db, and the
# .mbsyncstate files of SyncState *). Such folders are not synced; see
# docs/troubleshooting.md. The last matching pattern wins.
# Sync Pull — one direction only, never pushes changes back to Proton.
# SyncState * — each folder's sync state (UIDVALIDITY and UIDs) lives in
#   its own Maildir directory as .mbsyncstate*, so it survives container
#   restarts and two folders can never share a state file. A path prefix
#   such as /maildir/.mbsyncstate would name each file after the folder
#   with "/" written as "!", so Folders/A/B and Folders/A!B would collide
#   (#275).
# Expunge None — never permanently deletes on either side.
# CopyArrivalDate yes — a new near file's mtime is the message's IMAP
#   INTERNALDATE, the arrival time Bridge reports, instead of the time
#   mbsync wrote the file (#1081). Read-side only: it adds INTERNALDATE
#   to each FETCH and sets the time on the file isync writes; with Sync
#   Pull nothing is stored on the far side. Files synced before the
#   option keep the mtime of their first sync (docs/architecture.md,
#   "Message time").
Channel protonmail
Far :protonmail-remote:
Near :protonmail-local:
Patterns * !"All Mail" !"Labels/*" !"Starred" !"*/uidvalidity" !"*/uidvalidity/*" !"*/isyncuidmap.db" !"*/isyncuidmap.db/*" !"*/mbsyncstate" !"*/mbsyncstate/*" !"*/mbsyncstate.journal" !"*/mbsyncstate.journal/*" !"*/mbsyncstate.new" !"*/mbsyncstate.new/*" !"*/mbsyncstate.lock" !"*/mbsyncstate.lock/*"
Create Near
SyncState *
Sync Pull
Expunge None
CopyArrivalDate yes
